.TH "OSTRYA\-SUMMARY" "1" "" "ostrya" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ostrya\-summary \- Regenerate, sign, or verify the repository summary
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ostrya summary [OPTIONS] [KEY_ID]...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Regenerate, sign, or verify the repository summary.
.PP
With \%\-u, the command writes the root summary \fB\%summary\fR, which holds each ref, and the summary of each auth item of the client groups, from the refs of the repository. It signs each one with the given keys. With \%\-u \%\-c, it writes the summaries of the auth items alone, and leaves \fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR as they are. With \%\-\-verify, it verifies the signatures in \fB\%summary.sig\fR, and \%\-\-verify takes precedence over \%\-u. Without \%\-u and \%\-\-verify, a \%KEY\-ID or a \%\-\-keys\-file signs each existing summary. The read options \%\-\-raw, \%\-v, \%\-\-list\-metadata\-keys, and \%\-\-print\-metadata\-key print the local summary, or with \%\-\-auth\-group the summary of the item of one client group. If no option gives an action, the command exits \%1.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fIKEY_ID...\fR
Signing or verification keys, in the format of \fB\%ostrya sign\fR\&.
.IP
This is an ostrya extension. With \%\-u, the keys sign each summary after every \%\-\-sign key. Without \%\-u, they sign each existing summary, and the command then ignores every read option. With \%\-\-verify, they are the trusted keys.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-update\fR
Regenerate the root summary and the summary of each auth item from the refs of the repository.
.IP
The root summary \fB\%summary\fR holds each ref, whatever the client groups. An auth item has a summary when one or more of its client groups has \fB\%summary=true\fR\&. With no client group, the \fB\%anonymous\fR item has a summary that holds each ref. The summary of an item holds the refs that the item reads in each namespace whose selected group has \fB\%summary=true\fR\&. A namespace whose selected group has \fB\%summary=false\fR adds no ref. The summary of each item, \fB\%anonymous\fR included, is under \fB\%extensions/ostrya/summary/\fR\&. The root summary states the mode of the repository. The summary of an item states \fB\%archive\-z2\fR, the mode that \fB\%ostrya serve\fR serves. In a mode other than \fB\%archive\fR, the summary of an item names no static delta. The command writes each summary and replaces its signature file. Each summary gets a signature of each \%\-\-gpg\-sign key, each \%\-\-sign key, and each \%KEY\-ID and \%\-\-keys\-file key, in this order. The command writes the \fB\%\&.access\fR file of each item last, and removes the files of each item that has no summary.
.IP
The command checks every \%\-m value, every key, and the client groups before the regeneration, so a refusal leaves each summary as it was. For each directory under \fB\%refs/mirrors/\fR whose name is not a collection id, the command writes one \fB\%warning:\fR line to standard error. It prints nothing else. With \%\-c, the command does not write the root summary.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-client\-groups\fR
With \%\-u, write the summaries of the auth items alone.
.IP
This is an ostrya extension. The command writes and signs the summary of each auth item under \fB\%extensions/ostrya/summary/\fR, and removes the files of each item that has no summary, as \%\-u does. It leaves \fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR in the repository root as they are. In a repository with a collection id, it writes no \fB\%ostree\-metadata\fR anchor commit. The option needs \%\-u.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-view\fR
View a report of the local summary file.
.IP
The report holds each ref, the refs of each collection, and the global metadata. This option takes precedence over \%\-\-list\-metadata\-keys and \%\-\-print\-metadata\-key. If the repository has no \fB\%summary\fR file, the command exits \%1.
.TP
\fB\-\-raw\fR
View the local summary file in the GVariant text form.
.IP
This option takes precedence over every other read option.
.TP
\fB\-\-list\-metadata\-keys\fR
List the available metadata keys.
.IP
The command prints the keys of the global metadata, in sorted order.
.TP
\fB\-\-print\-metadata\-key\fR \fIKEY\fR
Print the value of one metadata key.
.IP
If the global metadata has no such key, the command exits \%1.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-add\-metadata\fR \fIKEY=VALUE\fR
Add a metadata field to the summary, as \%KEY=VALUE.
.IP
VALUE is in the GVariant text form. The option can occur more than once. Only \%\-u reads it. If the summary writes KEY as one of its own entries, the summary keeps its own value. In a mode other than \fB\%archive\fR, the summary of an auth item drops \fB\%ostree.static\-deltas\fR and \fB\%ostree.summary.indexed\-deltas\fR\&. A repository with a collection id refuses this option.
.TP
\fB\-\-sign\fR \fIKEY\-ID\fR
Sign the summary with this key.
.IP
The \%\-\-sign\-type option selects the engine. For \%ed25519 and spki, the key is the \%base64 of the secret key. An spki secret key is a \%PKCS#8 DER, a \%SEC1 DER that names the \%P\-256 curve, or a \%PKCS#1 DER of an RSA key, and its \%base64 can hold line breaks. For gpg, the key is a selector, as for \%\-\-gpg\-sign. Each occurrence adds one signature. Only \%\-u reads it.
.TP
\fB\-\-gpg\-sign\fR \fIKEY\-ID\fR
Sign the summary with the GPG key that this selector names.
.IP
The option uses the gpg engine for each \%\-\-sign\-type. Each occurrence adds one signature. Only \%\-u reads it.
.TP
\fB\-\-verify\fR
Verify the signatures of the summary.
.IP
The command then does not regenerate or sign the summary, also with \%\-u. It prints one line for each signature and then \fB\%verification OK\fR or \fB\%verification FAILED\fR\&. If no signature is valid, the command exits \%1. The \%ed25519 and spki engines read the key sources of \fB\%ostrya sign \-\-verify\fR\&. The gpg engine reads each \%\-\-keys\-file keyring, or else the keyrings of \%\-\-remote, or else the global trusted set.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-sign\-type\fR \fINAME\fR
The signature engine for \%\-\-verify, \%\-\-sign, and the positional keys.
.IP
The engine also applies to \%\-\-keys\-file. The names are \fB\%ed25519\fR, \fB\%spki\fR, and \fB\%gpg\fR, and the command refuses any other name. The default is \fB\%ed25519\fR\&. If the option occurs more than once, the last value wins.
.TP
\fB\-\-last\-modified\fR \fILAST_MODIFIED\fR
Set \fB\%ostree.summary.last\-modified\fR, in seconds since the Unix epoch.
.IP
A fixed value makes the output reproducible. Without this option, the command uses the current time. \fB\%SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH\fR does not change this value. Only \%\-u reads it.
.TP
\fB\-\-metadata\-commit\-timestamp\fR \fIMETADATA_COMMIT_TIMESTAMP\fR
The timestamp of the collection anchor commit, in Unix epoch seconds.
.IP
The anchor commit is the \fB\%ostree\-metadata\fR commit. Without this option, the timestamp is \fB\%SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH\fR if it is set, or else the current time. Only \%\-u without \%\-c reads it, and only a repository with a collection id uses it.
.TP
\fB\-\-keys\-file\fR \fIPATH\fR
Read keys from a file, in the format of \fB\%ostrya sign\fR\&.
.IP
For spki, a signing file holds PEM \fB\%PRIVATE KEY\fR blocks, and a verify file holds PEM \fB\%PUBLIC KEY\fR blocks. For an \%ed25519 or spki verify, the last value wins. With this option or a \%KEY\-ID, that verify does not read \%\-\-keys\-dir or the system key directories. A signing run reads every file. A gpg verify reads every file as a keyring, and a gpg signing run refuses this option. Without \%\-u and \%\-\-verify, the keys sign the existing summary.
.TP
\fB\-\-keys\-dir\fR \fIPATH\fR
Read the keys of an \%ed25519 or spki verify from this directory.
.IP
The verify reads \fB\%trusted.<type>\fR, \fB\%revoked.<type>\fR, and the files in \fB\%trusted.<type>.d/\fR and \fB\%revoked.<type>.d/\fR\&. It then does not read the system key directories \fB\%/etc/ostree\fR and \fB\%/usr/share/ostree\fR\&. An \%ed25519 file has one \%base64 key per line, and an spki file has PEM \fB\%PUBLIC KEY\fR blocks. If the option occurs more than once, the last value wins. An empty value names the working directory. A gpg verify refuses this option.
.TP
\fB\-\-gpg\-homedir\fR \fIGPG_HOMEDIR\fR
The GnuPG home directory where gpg resolves signing keys.
.IP
Only the gpg engine reads it: for \%\-\-gpg\-sign, and for \%\-\-sign and the positional keys under the gpg engine.
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\fR \fIREMOTE\fR
Add the trusted gpg keyrings of this remote to a gpg verify.
.IP
The keyrings are \fB\%<remote>.trustedkeys.gpg\fR in the repository and in \fB\%/etc/ostree/remotes.d/\fR, in addition to the global trusted set. With \%\-\-keys\-file, the command does not read them. Only the gpg engine reads this option.
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\-group\fR \fINAME\fR
Read the summary of the auth item of the client group NAME.
.IP
This is an ostrya extension. It applies to \%\-v, \%\-\-raw, \%\-\-list\-metadata\-keys, and \%\-\-print\-metadata\-key. The command reads the file of the summary of the item, and does not compare its \fB\%\&.access\fR file with the client groups. In a repository with no client group, NAME is \fB\%anonymous\fR or \fB\%anonymous\-collections\fR, the implicit groups. The command refuses a group that does not exist, a group with \fB\%summary=false\fR, and a group whose \fB\%auth\fR list holds more than one item. It also refuses this option with \%\-u, with \%\-\-verify, with a signing key, and with no read option.
.PP
The global options are listed in \fBostrya\fR(1).
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBostrya\fR(1)
